WON'T FIX: a link reference definition whose LABEL wraps across lines is
dropped, and its use is resolved inline. The project accepts this and does not
plan to fix it.

WHY WON'T FIX. Wrapping a link label across lines is rare, and the correct fix
is not contained. Both recovery grammars read a label from ONE line, so BOTH
must learn multi-line labels together. Extending only the definition side would
recover the definition while the link still resolves inline, duplicating the
URL — the trap defect 27 documents. A multi-line-label parser must also stay
strict-or-equal to cmark (reject a blank line inside a label, honour cmark's
length cap). For how rarely the shape appears, that cost is not worth paying, so
the current behaviour stands.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The whole output is one line:

    Uses [foo bar](/url).

The definition is gone and the reference spelling with it.

WHY THE EXPECTATION STAYS CORRECT. expected.md holds what a fix WOULD produce —
both kept byte for byte, like every single-line definition: the wrapped
`[foo\nbar]: /url` block and the `[foo bar]` reference. Keeping it means the
decision is reversible, and if the behaviour ever changes this case turns green
and the runner reports an unexpected pass — the signal to reclassify it as
fixed rather than let a stale marker hide the change.

CAUSE. `definitionLabel(inLine:)` scans each line of an uncovered span for
`[label]:`, so `[foo` on the first line yields no label, the span fails
`isDefinitionShaped`, and the definition is never written back. With no
recovered label, `restoreReferenceStyleLinks` has nothing to match and the
`[foo bar]` link keeps cmark's pasted-URL form. The folded-paragraph scanner
`leadingDefinitionLineCount` is single-line-label in the same way.

NOT A REGRESSION. The same input WITH a blank line fails the same way, so it
predates the folded-definition work and was found during it (defect 28).
